From 3032e84c7c1e790f9e0bab7b790b7304bb123466 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 18:32:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: bump music sample rate to 22050Hz

Direct user listening on the mono-audio round (patch 0022) confirmed
it: "music sounds much better, quality is obviously a bit off, but
better" -- real, working, but genuinely lower-fidelity than desired.
Asked whether 22050Hz (halfway between the current 11025Hz and
Passage's original 44100Hz default) was feasible.

Real-hardware data made this a much easier call than it would have
been before patch 0022 landed: the mono fix's real-hardware
confirmation (486DX2-50, PUMPDBG.LOG) showed the audio-pump loop
dropping to near-zero cost in steady state (avg_iters=0.000000 across
nearly every 20-frame window, not just "half of ~20-25ms" as
predicted) -- there's real headroom before doubling the sample rate
(and therefore the pump loop's per-chunk byte count) becomes a genuine
fps regression again. Still measuring rather than assuming: this is a
real, deliberate quality-vs-cost tradeoff, not a free change --
expected to cost some of the fps mono just bought back, in exchange for
roughly doubling the audible frequency range mono+11025Hz was limited
to.

sampleRate 11025 -> 22050 (musicPlayer.cpp global, used both for the
offline render and to request the physical device's rate via
SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate() -- so the 44100Hz-floor resample stays
eliminated regardless of which of the three rates in this port's
history gets used). Regenerated music/SONG.WAV via the render tool
(still mono, patch 0022's downmix step unchanged) -- 5,997,600 bytes,
exactly double the 11025Hz-mono file, and coincidentally the same total
byte count as the very first 11025Hz-stereo render (same
duration*rate*channels*bytesPerSample product, sanity-confirms the
math).

Verified under DOSBox-X: builds; WAV loads and plays without error;
boots, plays, exits cleanly via ESCAPE, no hang; PUMPDBG.LOG shows the
same "settles to near-zero after a few startup windows" pattern as the
mono-at-11025Hz build, mechanism confirmed working -- real magnitude
(and whether the fps trade is worth it) needs real hardware. As with
patch 0022, cannot verify actual audible quality/correctness from this
sandboxed environment -- real-hardware confirmation (fps, PUMPDBG.LOG,
and a genuine listening judgment on whether 22050Hz sounds
meaningfully better than 11025Hz to justify whatever fps cost it turns
out to have) is the actual test.
---
 gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp |  32 +++++++++++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
index 1efd301..e555767 100644
--- a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
@@ -16,20 +16,26 @@
 #include <math.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 
-// DOS-PORT: 11025Hz, not 22050 (or Passage's original 44100 default).
+// DOS-PORT: 22050Hz, not 11025 (or Passage's original 44100 default).
 // Real-hardware measurement on the port's minimum-target CPU (486DX2-50)
-// found the SB PCM device-rate mismatch a major cost: SDL floors the
-// physical device to 44100Hz by default regardless of what's requested,
-// forcing a 4x logical->physical resample on every audio-ring pump and
-// proportionally more real-time-audio-per-pump-call needed at a higher
-// content rate. doskutsu's own production config (confirmed via their
-// source, not guessed) deliberately runs its SB16 device at 11025Hz
-// specifically because each pumped chunk then covers ~4x more real-time
-// audio than at 44100Hz, needing ~4x fewer pump calls to keep pace with
-// a frame interval -- combined with SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate()
-// below (forcing the physical device to actually open at this rate
-// instead of the 44100 floor), this eliminates the resampler entirely.
-int sampleRate = 11025;
+// originally found the SB PCM device-rate mismatch a major cost: SDL
+// floors the physical device to 44100Hz by default regardless of what's
+// requested, forcing a 4x logical->physical resample on every audio-ring
+// pump and proportionally more real-time-audio-per-pump-call needed at a
+// higher content rate. 11025Hz (doskutsu's own production rate, avoiding
+// that resample entirely via SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate() below)
+// fixed the fps regression but was later judged too low-fidelity by
+// direct listening (2026-08-27, after mono playback -- patches 0007-
+// 0009/0022 -- was confirmed a real fps win with acceptable quality).
+// 22050Hz is a real, deliberate quality-vs-cost tradeoff, not a default
+// worth assuming either way: it doubles the audio-pump loop's per-frame
+// byte count for the same real-time duration versus 11025Hz (roughly
+// doubling its measured cost, unconfirmed until the next real-hardware
+// round), while halving Nyquist-limited fidelity loss versus 44100Hz.
+// Still forces the physical device to open at this exact rate via
+// SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate() below, so the 44100-floor resample
+// stays eliminated regardless of which of these three rates is chosen.
+int sampleRate = 22050;
 
 
 
